    "body": "## Description\n\n`TokenizerConfig(trust_remote_code=True)` comes back with `trust_remote_code=False`. So do `tokenizer_hf_no_use_fast` and `tokenizer_hf_no_include_special_tokens`, which are the three fields the deprecation warning two lines further down tells people to use instead of `hf_tokenizer_kwargs`.\n\n`src/megatron/bridge/training/tokenizers/config.py:83-97`:\n\n```python\n        # HuggingFace tokenizer kwargs\n        self.tokenizer_hf_no_use_fast = not self.hf_tokenizer_kwargs.get(\"use_fast\", True)\n        self.tokenizer_hf_no_include_special_tokens = not self.hf_tokenizer_kwargs.get(\"include_special_tokens\", True)\n        self.trust_remote_code = self.hf_tokenizer_kwargs.get(\"trust_remote_code\", False)\n        if self.hf_tokenizer_kwargs:\n            warn_rank_0(\n                \"`hf_tokenizer_kwargs` is deprecated and will be removed soon. \"\n                \"Please, use `tokenizer_hf_no_use_fast` / `tokenizer_hf_no_include_special_tokens` / \"\n                \"`trust_remote_code` arguments directly instead.\"\n            )\n```\n\nThe three assignments sit outside the `if self.hf_tokenizer_kwargs:` guard, and the dict defaults to empty, so every construction rewrites them from the `.get` fallbacks. `build_tokenizer` then hands MCore a config in which those three are the fallbacks whatever the caller asked for, and `megatron/core/tokenizers/utils/build_tokenizer.py` turns them straight into `use_fast`, `trust_remote_code` and `include_special_tokens` for `AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained`.\n\nMeasured. The child `__post_init__` is the real file on `main` and the parent dataclass is the real source text of `megatron.training.config.TokenizerConfig`, lifted out with `ast` because importing it pulls all of `megatron.core` (python 3.12 and torch, neither of which I have here):\n\n```\n  asked trust_remote_code                        True -> got False\n  asked tokenizer_hf_no_use_fast                 True -> got False\n  asked tokenizer_hf_no_include_special_tokens   True -> got False\n  asked tokenizer_sentencepiece_legacy           True -> got True\n  hf_tokenizer_kwargs=None -> AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get'\n```\n\nThe fourth line is the control. `tokenizer_sentencepiece_legacy` had the same clobber and lost it in #5998, and `test_config_success` has asserted since then that it survives construction. The hf trio was left behind.\n\n`include_special_tokens` is the quiet one, since it changes text to id conversion with nothing in the logs. `use_fast` silently swaps the slow tokenizer for the fast one. `trust_remote_code` turns into a confusing transformers-side failure for a repo with custom tokenizer code, and on the Megatron-LM checkpoint path it also makes the guard at `model_load_save.py:236-240` unreachable, since the value it inspects has already been forced to False.\n\nAlso reachable from `mlm_compat/arguments.py:53-66`, which copies every matching field out of a checkpoint's saved argparse namespace into `TokenizerConfig(**kw_args)` and so loses whatever the checkpoint recorded for those three.\n\n### The change\n\nRead a key only when the deprecated dict actually carries it, which is what `_resolve_hf_tokenizer_revision` in `tokenizers/tokenizer.py:112-119` already does:\n\n```python\n    if \"use_fast\" in hf_tokenizer_kwargs:\n        resolved_config.tokenizer_hf_no_use_fast = not hf_tokenizer_kwargs[\"use_fast\"]\n```\n\nMoving the reads inside the existing guard also stops `hf_tokenizer_kwargs=None`, which the annotation at `config.py:47` allows, from reaching `.get`. `tokenizer.py:90` already normalises that with `config.hf_tokenizer_kwargs or {}`.\n\nAfter:\n\n```\n  asked trust_remote_code                        True -> got True\n  asked tokenizer_hf_no_use_fast                 True -> got True\n  asked tokenizer_hf_no_include_special_tokens   True -> got True\n  asked tokenizer_sentencepiece_legacy           True -> got True\n  hf_tokenizer_kwargs=None -> ok\n\ndeprecated dict still wins when it carries the key:\n  use_fast=False in dict         -> tokenizer_hf_no_use_fast   = True\n  trust_remote_code=True in dict -> trust_remote_code          = True\n  include_special_tokens absent  -> no_include_special_tokens  = True\n```\n\n## Tests\n\nThree cases in `TestTokenizerConfig` in `tests/unit_tests/training/test_config.py`, beside the `test_config_success` that already pins the sentencepiece sibling: the direct fields survive, a key the deprecated dict supplies still wins while one it omits leaves the field alone, and `None` is accepted. The first fails on `main` on all three asserts, the third raises `AttributeError` there.\n\n`test_config_success` keeps passing unchanged, since it exercises the non-empty-dict path.\n\n## What I could not run here\n\n`requires-python = \">=3.12,<3.13\"` and the suite needs torch and Transformer Engine, and this is a python 3.11 machine with no GPU, so I have not run `uv run python -m pytest tests/unit_tests/training/test_config.py`. The transcripts above come from the real `__post_init__` and the real parent dataclass with nothing else stubbed, and the test bodies are plain dataclass construction over the same three fields.\n\n`ruff` 0.9.9 check and format are clean on both files.\n\n## Labels\n\nPer CONTRIBUTING this wants `bug` and `area:training`. I cannot set labels on this repository, so if someone with triage rights could add them it would stop the PR sitting in the queue.\n",
